In this work, we depart from the conventional approach to predicting runtime in HPC systems at the system-level and motivate modeling at the user-level. To improve performance, we embed historical behavior into the model by combining past runtimes conditioned on requested queue, wall time, and nodes with current submissions resulting in regime-aware user-profiles. Compared to a static model of Argonne National Laboratory’s Aurora exascale HPC system, we achieve a reduction in median absolute error, median normalized error, and underprediction rate up to 98.14%, 98.21%, and 44.22%, respectively.